[Music]  all right thank you Malcolm X when he  got up to speak places you see sometimes  say I see some friends and I even see  some enemies and I'm glad to stay I only  see friends here glad to see so many of  you all here today I've been asked to  speak around the issue of disarmament  and building bombs versus building  houses and housing and so I stand before  you today to declare that instead of  building bombs we need to build a  housing affordable housing yes  affordable housing in good neighborhoods  with well-funded schools  plenty of public park space and street  lights that come on when the Sun Goes  Down see I had the privilege of growing  up in a neighborhood like that in the  80s and 90s and ever West Side of  Manhattan in New York City I lived on  the fourth floor of a 12-story pre-war  building and for the younger folks here  pre-war men pre-world War Two was built  in 1927 pre-world War two apartment  building that sat on Riverside Drive and  looked out over the Hudson River I could  come out the front door of the apartment  building and walk across the street to  the Riverside Park I will go down there  and play Little League and spend  countless hours over the years that I  grew up what you need to know is I was  raised by a single mother who taught  elementary school-aged children the New  York City Public Schools for over 35  years and she raised me and my older  sister with little to no help from  either of our fathers and without the  kind of resources that would have  allowed her to stay in a neighborhood  like that if it wasn't for this  ingenious invention called rent  regulations rent regulations my mother  moved into the fourth floor  of that apartment building in the late  1960s and she is still there to this day  in that same apartment in New York City  there are two categories of rent  regulated apartments some are in the  category of rent controlled which means  that whatever the wrench is when you  move into that apartment building is  what the rent is today and forever  and those that are rent stabilized which  means the rent can only go up by small  and modest increments every year that  apartment has been rent stabilized that  she has lived in since the late 1960s  now unlike what I will call I hope it  doesn't offend anybody the tragic  failure of the renter coalition here in  the Commonwealth of Massachusetts in the  1990s to keep rent regulations from  being lifted in the Commonwealth in New  York City those rental coalitions are  strong and they protect what is becoming  an increasingly modest supply of rent  regulated apartments that remain as  tenants die out and there is no family  member to assume their leash because in  New York City there is a thing called  secession and you can secede your family  members lease to rent control to rent  stabilized apartments what I'm here to  tell you today is that I am of the  belief that it is time for Boston time  for Cambridge and time for every other  economically diverse community in this  area and in this country to get serious  about building affordable housing  protecting affordable housing and making  it possible for every young kid no  matter how marginal their circumstances  to grow up like I did in a good  neighborhood with well funded schools  penny of public park space and lights  that come on at night on the streets  as a matter of moral justice those  communities should not be limited to the  white wealthy to the privileged or to  the rich and at this stage of the game  what I have referred to as the final  stages of gentrification in a place like  Cambridge that is who they are limited  to white the wealthy the privileged and  the rich the reality is that well-funded  public schools bring opportunity publics  park spaces bring activities and fond  memories and street lights that come on  when the Sun Goes Down  after safety and security and stability  in neighborhoods so affordable housing  is really about more than giving a man a  home as Ben Harper sings it is about  opportunity it is about the possibility  for equality and it is about providing  everyone with a chance to flourish in  life as the effort is like to see and so  we have to get serious about  articulating this wider vision for why  affordable housing is a moral imperative  a moral issue not a partisan issue not a  Republican or Democrat a right or left  issue but a moral issue and we need to  get serious about executing that vision  by as Malcolm X would say whatever means  unnecessary in order to get it done  we've got to stop fooling around trying  to negotiate with developers for a few  more units here and a few more units  there in the buildings that they put up  to make a killing when they start  charging $1,500 a month for 800 square  feet that I could turn around in twice  maybe we've got to stop fooling around  trying to leverage community benefits  agreement money that somehow seems to  have a hard time making a real tense in  the issue an abundant supply of  affordable housing is not something that  we should  be asking for as a favor but instead  demanding as a right for all Americans  native-born or otherwise so while Trump  rattle sabers with his alter ego in  North Korea Kim jong-un we need to be  insisting on keeping our priorities  straight here at home it is time to  build housing and not bombs it is time  to make my reality my childhood on the  Upper West Side of Manhattan in a  neighborhood with well-funded public  schools and plenty of public park space  and streetlights that came on at night  is time to make my childhood and my  reality not the exception for people who  aren't white rich privileged or wealthy  but the norm for every child in this  country in every community in every  neighborhood around America thank you  [Applause]  [Music]

